Mathematical Foundations of Physical Agency Under Uncertainty

A Contract-Based Composition Theory for Estimation, Propagation, Safe Action, and Closed-Loop Verification

Author: Ouadi Maakoul + AI Collaborative Research
Document type: Theoretical monograph
Version: 1.3 — Action-Availability Closure
Status: No experimental validation. All results are conditional on explicit contracts.

---

Abstract

We develop a mathematical framework for closed-loop safety of a physical system operating under state, parameter, and observation uncertainty. The framework is organised around four maps — true dynamics F^\star, certified propagation \widehat{F}, measurement contraction \mathcal{E}, and safe-action map \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} — composed through a discrete-time update on a feasibility set \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{Z}.

The central result is a contract-based composition theorem: under a set of primitive contracts (C0, C0′, C1, C2, C4, C5, C6), the true state Z_t^\star remains, with probability at least 1-\Delta, inside a safe set \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} for all t \le T, where \Delta is a pre-allocated safety budget.

We further establish a structural monotonicity theorem,

\mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1),

and a quantitative refinement bounding the agency loss \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)) - \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2)) by a modulus of the Lipschitz regularity of the CBF family. This constitutes the framework's principal mathematical content: an order-theoretic and metric bridge between estimation quality and physical agency.

The composition logic is closed under the stated contracts. The contracts themselves are hypotheses about a specific physical instantiation and are not derived here.

