2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Measurable Spaces

Let (\mathcal{Z}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{Z}}), (\mathcal{U}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{U}}), (\mathcal{W}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{W}}), (\mathcal{Y}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{Y}}) be measurable spaces. Let 2^{\mathcal{Z}} denote the power set.

Definition 2.1 (Set-valued map). A map g : \mathcal{X} \to 2^{\mathcal{Y}} is measurable if for every B \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{Y}}, the set \{x \in \mathcal{X} : g(x) \cap B \neq \emptyset\} is measurable.

2.2 Product Uncertainty Space

Definition 2.2 (Uncertainty space).

\Xi := \Theta_{\mathrm{epi}} \times \mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{R},

where \Theta_{\mathrm{epi}} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p, \mathcal{W} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^q, and \mathcal{R} \subset \mathfrak{se}(3) is the registration-error set.

Principle 2.1 (Epistemic–aleatory distinction).

· \theta \in \Theta_{\mathrm{epi}} is epistemic: its uncertainty is reducible by information acquisition.
· w \in \mathcal{W} is aleatory: its variability is not eliminated by identifying fixed parameters.

2.3 Control Barrier Functions (Discrete-Time)

Definition 2.2 (Barrier function and safe set). A function h : \mathcal{Z} \to \mathbb{R} defines a safe set

\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} := \{Z \in \mathcal{Z} : h(Z) \ge 0\}.

Definition 2.3 (Robust discrete-time CBF condition). Given \gamma \in (0,1), the admissible CBF action set at Z \in \mathcal{Z} is

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) := \left\{ u \in \mathcal{U} : \inf_{w \in \mathcal{W}} \bigl[ h(F^\star(Z,u,w)) - (1-\gamma) h(Z) \bigr] \ge 0 \right\}.

Remark 2.1. The parameter \gamma is the CBF contraction rate. It is not the conformal error \alpha_t; notation has been separated to avoid collision.

Definition 2.4 (Robust safe-action map — revised). For a set \mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{Z},

\boxed{\;\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}) := \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z), \qquad \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\emptyset) := \mathcal{U}.\;}

The intersection runs over the safe part of \mathcal{A} only. The convention \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\emptyset) = \mathcal{U} is the standard empty-intersection convention.

Remark 2.2 (Motivation for the restriction). If Z \notin \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, then h(Z) < 0 and the CBF inequality h(F^\star(Z,u,w)) \ge (1-\gamma) h(Z) is weaker, not stronger, since (1-\gamma) h(Z) < 0. Restricting the intersection to \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} eliminates the spurious relaxation. This makes the safe-action map sound without requiring the entire feasibility set \mathcal{Z}_t to lie in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Proposition 2.5 (Invariance interpretation). If \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \neq \emptyset and u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}), then

F^\star(\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, u, \mathcal{W}) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Proof. For Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, h(Z) \ge 0. Since u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z),

h(F^\star(Z,u,w)) \ge (1-\gamma) h(Z) \ge 0,

for every w \in \mathcal{W}, because 1-\gamma > 0. Hence F^\star(Z,u,w) \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. □

Corollary 2.6 (Monotonicity of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} under \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}). If \mathcal{A}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2, then \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_1).

Proof. \mathcal{A}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, hence \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{A}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) \subseteq \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{A}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z). □

