5. Primitive Contracts

The following contracts are the axioms of the framework. They are not theorems; they are the interface between the framework and a specific physical instantiation.

C0 — Initial Containment and Safety

Deterministic form.

\boxed{Z_0^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_0 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.}

Probabilistic form. If initialization is uncertain,

\boxed{P\bigl(Z_0^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_0 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}\bigr) \ge 1 - \alpha_0.}

C0′ — Initial Action Availability

\boxed{\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_0) \neq \emptyset.}

Remark 5.1. C0 does not imply C0′, because \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_0) may be empty even if Z_0^\star is safe. C0′ is the bootstrap condition that starts the induction. Under the probabilistic mode, C0′ may be stated as P(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_0) \neq \emptyset) \ge 1 - \alpha_0', with \alpha_0' folded into the safety budget.

C1 — Propagation Soundness

\boxed{F^\star(\mathcal{Z}, u, \mathcal{W}) \subseteq \widehat{F}(\mathcal{Z}, u), \qquad \forall \mathcal{Z} \in 2^{\mathcal{Z}}, \forall u \in \mathcal{U}.}

Here F^\star(\mathcal{Z}, u, \mathcal{W}) := \{F^\star(Z, u, w) : Z \in \mathcal{Z}, w \in \mathcal{W}\}.

C2 — Estimation Soundness

Bounded deterministic mode. With \eta_t \in \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{obs}} for a bounded set \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{obs}} and for all t \le T,

\boxed{Y_t \in H(Z_t) \oplus \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{obs}} \;\Longrightarrow\; Z_t \in \mathcal{E}(Y_t).}

Probabilistic mode. With \eta_t \sim P_\eta,

\boxed{P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{E}(Y_t) \mid A_E\bigr) \ge 1 - \beta_t,}

where A_E is the standing assumption under which the observation model is valid.

C3 — Safe-Action Soundness

Now a derived proposition, not a primitive contract. See Proposition 6.2 below. C3 is retained in the contract list only as a reference point for readers of v1.2.

C4 — Recursive Feasibility

\boxed{\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_t) \neq \emptyset \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{t+1}) \neq \emptyset.}

C5 — Probabilistic Containment

Let A := A_{\mathrm{CP}} \cap A_E be the standing event under which both the conformal-calibration and estimation assumptions hold.

\boxed{P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t \mid A\bigr) \ge 1 - \alpha_t.}

Here \alpha_t already includes estimation failure; C2 (probabilistic) is a sufficient decomposition of \alpha_t, not a separate budget line. If \widehat{F} or \mathcal{E} is itself only probabilistically valid, \alpha_t absorbs the corresponding error term.

C6 — Risk Accounting

\boxed{\alpha_0 + \alpha_0' + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \alpha_t \le \Delta.}

If the standing event A can fail with probability P(A^c) \le \rho, the unconditional bound is 1 - \Delta - \rho. If \beta_t is budgeted separately rather than absorbed into \alpha_t, the budget becomes

\alpha_0 + \alpha_0' + \sum_{t=1}^{T} (\alpha_t + \beta_t + \rho) \le \Delta.

Remark 5.2 (Seven primitive contracts). The primitive contracts are C0, C0′, C1, C2, C4, C5, C6 — seven in total. C3 has been demoted to a derived proposition.

