8. Probabilistic Composition Theorem

8.1 Preliminaries

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P) be a probability space on which the stochastic processes (Z_t^\star), (Y_t), (\eta_t) are defined. Let

A := A_{\mathrm{CP}} \cap A_E

be the single standing event under which both the conformal-calibration and estimation assumptions hold.

Definition 8.1 (Failure events).

\mathcal{G}_0 := \{Z_0^\star \notin \mathcal{Z}_0 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}\}, \qquad
\mathcal{F}_t := \{Z_t^\star \notin \mathcal{Z}_t\}, \qquad t \ge 1.

8.2 Main Theorem

Theorem 8.1 (Probabilistic Composition). Assume C0 (probabilistic), C0′, C1, C2 (probabilistic mode), C4, C5, C6. Then

\boxed{P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T \mid A\bigr) \ge 1 - \Delta.}

If P(A^c) \le \rho, the unconditional bound is 1 - \Delta - \rho.

Proof. Let \mathcal{G} := \mathcal{G}_0^c \cap \bigcap_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{F}_t^c be the good event. By the union bound over the marginals,

P(\mathcal{G}^c \mid A) \;\le\; P(\mathcal{G}_0 \mid A) + \sum_{t=1}^{T} P(\mathcal{F}_t \mid A) \;\le\; \alpha_0 + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \alpha_t \;\le\; \Delta,

where the middle inequality is C0 (probabilistic) for the first term and C5 for each subsequent term, and the last inequality is C6 (with \alpha_0' = 0 in the base form).

Pathwise argument on \mathcal{G}. Conditioned on \mathcal{G}, we have Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t for all t \le T. Run the induction of Theorem 7.1 pathwise:

· At t=0, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_0) \neq \emptyset by C0′.
· For t \ge 1, C4 gives \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_t) \neq \emptyset.
· Proposition 6.2 gives Z_{t+1}^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Therefore, on \mathcal{G}, Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} for all t \le T, and

P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T \mid A\bigr) \ge 1 - \Delta. \qquad \square

Remark 8.2 (Conditioning and the role of C2). The theorem conditions on the single standing event A = A_{\mathrm{CP}} \cap A_E. C2 is used pathwise only through the bounded-mode version when \eta_t \in \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{obs}} is deterministic; in the probabilistic mode, \beta_t is absorbed into \alpha_t (or budgeted separately if explicitly desired, in which case the union bound picks up an extra \sum_t \beta_t).

Remark 8.3 (Minimal budget via C2 alone). Since C1 is deterministic, the failure event \mathcal{F}_{t+1} satisfies

\mathcal{F}_{t+1} \subseteq \mathcal{F}_t \cup \{Z_{t+1}^\star \notin \mathcal{E}(Y_{t+1})\}.

If C2 (probabilistic) is available with rate \beta_t, the union bound yields

P\Bigl(\bigcup_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{F}_t \mid A\Bigr) \le \alpha_0 + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \beta_t,

with no conformal term. C5 is then needed only when \widehat{F} or \mathcal{E} is itself only probabilistically valid on a per-step basis.

