9. Structural Theorem: Action-Set Monotonicity and Its Quantitative Refinement

9.1 The Basic Monotonicity

Theorem 9.1 (Action-Set Monotonicity). Let \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} : 2^{\mathcal{Z}} \to 2^{\mathcal{U}} be defined as in Definition 2.4. Then for any \mathcal{Z}_1, \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z},

\boxed{\mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1).}

Proof. Suppose \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2. Then \mathcal{Z}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Taking intersections,

\bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) \;\subseteq\; \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z).

Hence \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1). □

Remark 9.2 (On triviality). Theorem 9.1 is a one-line consequence of intersection over a larger index set. Its content is conceptual: it formalises the direction in which estimation quality propagates into agency. The quantitative refinement below elevates it from an order-theoretic identity to a rate statement.

9.2 Agency Index

Definition 9.1 (Uncertainty-induced agency index). Given a reference set \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \subseteq \mathcal{Z} and a finite Borel measure \mu on \mathcal{U} with 0 < \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) < \infty, define

\boxed{\kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}) := 1 - \frac{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}))}{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}))}.}

Remark 9.3. For this to be well-defined, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) must be \Sigma_{\mathcal{U}}-measurable for every \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}. This is assumed. \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}} is not a metric: it fails symmetry, identity of indiscernibles, and the triangle inequality. It is a normalised index of uncertainty-induced agency loss.

Corollary 9.4 (Monotonicity and nonnegativity of the agency index). If \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, then

0 \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_2).

Proof. By Theorem 9.1, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1), so

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)).

Since \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \supseteq \mathcal{Z}_1, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}), so \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})). Hence \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \ge 0. The second inequality follows by substitution. □

9.3 Quantitative Refinement

We now strengthen Theorem 9.1 to a rate statement under explicit regularity assumptions.

Assumption 9.A (Lipschitz CBF family). There exist a constant L > 0 and a family \{g_Z\}_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}} of functions g_Z : \mathcal{U} \to \mathbb{R} such that:

(i) \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) = \{u \in \mathcal{U} : g_Z(u) \ge 0\} for every Z \in \mathcal{Z};
(ii) |g_Z(u) - g_{Z'}(u)| \le L \, d_{\mathcal{Z}}(Z, Z') for all Z, Z' \in \mathcal{Z} and all u \in \mathcal{U}.

Assumption 9.B (Level-set regularity). There exists \psi : [0,\infty) \to [0,\infty) nondecreasing with \psi(0) = 0 such that for every Z \in \mathcal{Z} and every t \ge 0,

\mu\bigl(\{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) : g_Z(u) < t\}\bigr) \le \psi(t).

Proposition 9.5 (Quantitative monotonicity). Under Assumptions 9.A and 9.B, for any \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}},

\boxed{0 \;\le\; \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z})) - \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) \;\le\; \psi\bigl(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr).}

Proof. Since \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, Theorem 9.1 gives \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}), so the difference is nonnegative.

For the upper bound, let u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}). Since u \notin \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}), there exists Z' \in \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} with g_{Z'}(u) < 0. Pick any Z \in \mathcal{Z} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}; such Z exists because \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \neq \emptyset is required for the difference to be positive. Since u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z), g_Z(u) \ge 0. By Assumption 9.A(ii),

0 \le g_Z(u) \le g_{Z'}(u) + L \, d_{\mathcal{Z}}(Z, Z') < L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}).

Thus

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \;\subseteq\; \{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) : g_Z(u) < L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\},

and by Assumption 9.B the measure of this set is at most \psi(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})). Hence

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z})) - \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) \le \psi\bigl(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr). \qquad \square

Remark 9.6. Proposition 9.5 turns Theorem 9.1 from a bare order-theoretic statement into a Lipschitz-type rate. When \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, the difference is zero; the bound \psi(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) is then not tight but remains valid. When \mathcal{Z} shrinks to a point, \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) remains the diameter of the reference set; a sharper bound would use \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \setminus \mathcal{Z}) and may be obtained by partitioning \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \setminus \mathcal{Z} into local charts.

Remark 9.7 (The framework's mathematical content). The chain

\text{better estimation} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{smaller } \mathcal{Z} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{larger } \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \;\longrightarrow\; \text{greater agency}

is the order-theoretic content of the phrase physical agency under uncertainty. Proposition 9.5 supplies the quantitative counterpart: agency loss is bounded by a modulus \psi of the Lipschitz constant of the CBF family applied to the diameter of the reference set.

