10. Reachability in Belief Space

10.1 Information-State Transition

Definition 10.1 (Information-state transition).

\mathfrak{I}_{t+1} = \mathcal{T}_{\mathfrak{I}}(\mathfrak{I}_t, u_t, Y_{t+1}, w_t, \epsilon_t^{\mathrm{reg}}),

defined componentwise:

\mathcal{Z}_{t+1} = \widehat{F}(\mathcal{Z}_t, u_t) \cap \mathcal{E}(Y_{t+1}),

b_{t+1}(z') \propto p(Y_{t+1} \mid z') \int_{\mathcal{Z}} T(z' \mid z, u_t) \, b_t(z) \, dz,

and \Theta_{t+1}, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{R} evolve according to their own dynamics.

10.2 Reachability Set

Definition 10.2 (Policy reachability).

\mathfrak{R}_0^\pi := \{\mathfrak{I}_0\},

\mathfrak{R}_{t+1}^\pi := \left\{ \mathcal{T}_{\mathfrak{I}}(\mathfrak{I}, u, Y, w, \epsilon) : \begin{array}{l} \mathfrak{I} \in \mathfrak{R}_t^\pi, \\ u = \pi(\mathfrak{I}), \\ w \in \mathcal{W}, \; \epsilon \in \mathcal{R}, \\ Y \in \mathcal{Y}(\mathfrak{I}, u, w, \epsilon) \end{array} \right\}.

Remark 10.1. The policy acts on the full information state, not on a partial tuple.

10.3 Uncertifiable Information States

Definition 10.3.

\mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} := \{\mathfrak{I} : \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}}) = \emptyset\}.

Proposition 10.4 (Reachability certificate). If

\mathfrak{R}^\pi(\mathfrak{I}_0, T) \cap \mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} = \emptyset,

then there exists an admissible safe action at every reachable information state, for all t \le T.

Proof. Direct from Definition 10.3: a reachable state not in \mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} has \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}}) \neq \emptyset. □

Remark 10.2. This is a certificate of recursive availability of an admissible safety action. It is not by itself a certificate of physical safety. Physical safety requires Proposition 6.2 plus the containment invariant of Theorem 7.1.

