11. Three Distinct Certificates

11.1 Certificate D (Deterministic Containment)

\boxed{\mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.}

Every state consistent with the estimate is safe. This is the strongest certificate.

11.2 Certificate P (Probabilistic Coverage)

\boxed{P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T\bigr) \ge 1 - \Delta.}

The true state is safe with high probability.

11.3 Certificate B (Belief Support)

\boxed{\operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.}

Every state in the belief support is safe. This is a geometric statement about b_t, not a probabilistic statement.

11.4 Relations

Proposition 11.1 (Corrected relations).

1. D \wedge \{Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t\} \Rightarrow Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Hence D \Rightarrow P with probability at least the containment probability 1 - \Delta_{\mathrm{cont}}.
2. D \Rightarrow B when \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_t.
3. B \not\Rightarrow P.
4. P \not\Rightarrow B.
5. P \not\Rightarrow D.
6. \boxed{\text{support inclusion} \neq \text{probabilistic calibration}.}

Proof.

1. Direct: \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t imply Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

2. Direct from \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

3. Take \mathcal{Z} = \mathbb{R}, \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} = [0, \infty), Z_t^\star \equiv -1, and b_t = \mathrm{Unif}[0,1]. Then \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, but P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 0. The true state is unsafe while the belief support is safe.

4. Take Z_t^\star \equiv 1 and b_t = \delta_{-1}. Then P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 1, but \operatorname{supp}(b_t) = \{-1\} \not\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

5. Take Z_t^\star \equiv 1 and \mathcal{Z}_t = \{1, -1\}. Then P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 1, but \mathcal{Z}_t \not\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. □

Remark 11.2. The distinction between B and P is essential. Belief-space safety literature frequently conflates the two, but a support inclusion says nothing about calibration, and a probabilistic guarantee says nothing about support.

