12. Falsification

12.1 Residual

For Euclidean components,

r_t := \hat{Z}_t^{\mathrm{measured}} - Z_t^{\mathrm{pred}}.

For \mathrm{SE}(3) components T \in \mathrm{SE}(3),

r_T := \mathrm{Log}\bigl( (T_t^{\mathrm{pred}})^{-1} \hat{T}_t \bigr) \in \mathfrak{se}(3),

with weighted norm

\|r_T\|_{W_T} := \sqrt{r_T^\top W_T \, r_T}.

12.2 Composite Residual Threshold

\varepsilon_{\mathrm{total}} := \bar{\omega} + L_\Phi \bar{e} + L_\theta \bar{\theta} + \bar{\eta},

where \bar{\omega} is the process-disturbance bound, \bar{e} the observation-error bound, \bar{\theta} the parameter-uncertainty bound, \bar{\eta} the sensor-noise bound, L_\Phi is a Lipschitz constant for the observation map, and L_\theta is a Lipschitz constant for the parameter dependence.

12.3 Falsification Criterion

Proposition 12.1.

\|r_t\| \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{total}} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{composite hypothesis } \mathrm{NotRejected}.

\|r_t\| > \varepsilon_{\mathrm{total}} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{composite hypothesis } \mathrm{Rejected}.

Remark 12.1. The word consistent is deliberately avoided. Surviving one test does not establish consistency in any statistical or philosophical sense. The composite hypothesis includes nominal model, error bounds, sensor calibration, registration model, and numerical implementation; rejection falsifies the composite, not the claim F \neq F^\star.

