Appendix C: Proof of Proposition 2.5 (Details)

Let Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, so h(Z) \ge 0. Let u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}), so

\inf_{w \in \mathcal{W}} [h(F^\star(Z, u, w)) - (1-\gamma) h(Z)] \ge 0.

Then for every w \in \mathcal{W},

h(F^\star(Z, u, w)) \ge (1-\gamma) h(Z) \ge 0,

because 1-\gamma > 0 and h(Z) \ge 0. Hence F^\star(Z, u, w) \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Since Z and w were arbitrary, F^\star(\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, u, \mathcal{W}) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. □

---

Appendix D: Proof of Corollary 9.4 (Details)

Assume \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}.

By Theorem 9.1 applied to \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2:

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1).

By monotonicity of the measure \mu:

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)).

By Theorem 9.1 applied to \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}:

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1),

so \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})). Dividing by \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) > 0:

\frac{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2))}{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}))} \le \frac{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1))}{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}))} \le 1.

Subtracting both sides from 1 and reversing:

0 \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_2). \qquad \square

