Appendix C: Full Proofs for Section 2

C.1 Proof of Proposition 2.5 (Invariance Interpretation)

Proposition 2.5. Let \mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{Z} with \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \neq \emptyset, and let u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}). Then

F^\star(\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, u, \mathcal{W}) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Proof. Let Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. By definition of \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}},

h(Z) \ge 0.

By definition of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}) = \bigcap_{Z' \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z'), we have u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z). By Definition 2.3 of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z),

\inf_{w \in \mathcal{W}} \bigl[ h(F^\star(Z, u, w)) - (1-\gamma) h(Z) \bigr] \ge 0.

Therefore, for every w \in \mathcal{W},

h(F^\star(Z, u, w)) \ge (1-\gamma) h(Z).

Since \gamma \in (0,1), we have 1 - \gamma > 0. Combining with h(Z) \ge 0,

h(F^\star(Z, u, w)) \ge (1-\gamma) h(Z) \ge 0.

Therefore F^\star(Z, u, w) \in \{Z' : h(Z') \ge 0\} = \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Since Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and w \in \mathcal{W} were arbitrary,

F^\star(\mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, u, \mathcal{W}) = \{F^\star(Z, u, w) : Z \in \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, w \in \mathcal{W}\} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. \qquad \square

C.2 Proof of Corollary 2.6 (Monotonicity of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}})

Corollary 2.6. If \mathcal{A}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}, then \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_1).

Proof. From \mathcal{A}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2 we obtain \mathcal{A}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Therefore

\bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{A}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) \;\subseteq\; \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{A}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z),

because intersection over a larger index set is contained in the intersection over a smaller index set. By Definition 2.4, this is precisely

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_1). \qquad \square

Remark C.1. Corollary 2.6 is the engine of Theorem 9.1. The refinement in Definition 2.4 (intersecting over \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} rather than \mathcal{A}) does not weaken the monotonicity: the map \mathcal{A} \mapsto \mathcal{A} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} is itself monotone.

C.3 On the Empty-Set Convention

Convention C.2. \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\emptyset) := \mathcal{U}.

Justification. The empty intersection is conventionally the whole universe. This convention is consistent with Corollary 2.6: if \mathcal{A}_1 = \emptyset \subseteq \mathcal{A}_2, then \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{A}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U} = \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\emptyset). It also ensures that \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} is total as a map 2^{\mathcal{Z}} \to 2^{\mathcal{U}}, avoiding partiality.

Remark C.3. The convention has no bearing on Theorem 7.1 because \mathcal{Z}_t \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \neq \emptyset is maintained by the induction (Corollary 7.2).

