Appendix F: Full Proof of Theorem 8.1 (Probabilistic Composition)

F.1 Setup

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P) be a probability space supporting the processes (Z_t^\star), (Y_t), (\eta_t). Let

A := A_{\mathrm{CP}} \cap A_E

be the standing event under which both the conformal calibration and the estimation model are valid. Assume P(A^c) \le \rho.

Define the failure events

\mathcal{G}_0 := \{Z_0^\star \notin \mathcal{Z}_0 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}\}, \qquad
\mathcal{F}_t := \{Z_t^\star \notin \mathcal{Z}_t\}, \quad t \ge 1,

and the good event

\mathcal{G} := \mathcal{G}_0^c \cap \bigcap_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{F}_t^c.

F.2 Proof of Theorem 8.1

Theorem 8.1. Assume C0 (probabilistic), C0′, C1, C2 (probabilistic mode), C4, C5, C6. Then

P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T \mid A\bigr) \ge 1 - \Delta.

If P(A^c) \le \rho, then unconditionally

P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T\bigr) \ge 1 - \Delta - \rho.

Proof.

Step 1: Union bound on the failure event.

We bound P(\mathcal{G}^c \mid A). By De Morgan's law,

\mathcal{G}^c = \mathcal{G}_0 \cup \bigcup_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{F}_t.

By the union bound (Boole's inequality),

P(\mathcal{G}^c \mid A) \;\le\; P(\mathcal{G}_0 \mid A) + \sum_{t=1}^{T} P(\mathcal{F}_t \mid A). \tag{F.1}

Step 2: Bounding each term.

For the first term, by C0 (probabilistic) with the standing event A,

P(\mathcal{G}_0 \mid A) \;\le\; P(\mathcal{G}_0) / P(A) \;\le\; \alpha_0 / P(A) \;\le\; \alpha_0 + \alpha_0'

when \alpha_0' absorbs the 1/P(A) correction. In the base form, assume P(A) = 1 or fold the correction into C6.

For each t \ge 1, C5 gives

P(\mathcal{F}_t \mid A) \;\le\; \alpha_t. \tag{F.2}

Substituting into (F.1),

P(\mathcal{G}^c \mid A) \;\le\; \alpha_0 + \alpha_0' + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \alpha_t \;\le\; \Delta, \tag{F.3}

where the last inequality is C6.

Step 3: Pathwise induction on the good event.

Condition on \mathcal{G}. By definition of \mathcal{G}, for every t \in \{0, 1, \dots, T\},

Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t \quad \text{and (for } t=0) \quad Z_0^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. \tag{F.4}

We run the induction of Theorem 7.1 pathwise.

· Base case. Z_0^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_0 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} by (F.4). Action availability \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_0) \neq \emptyset is supplied by C0′ (deterministic; if C0′ is probabilistic with rate \alpha_0', absorb into \Delta).
· Inductive step. Assume Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_t) \neq \emptyset. Select u_t = \pi(\mathfrak{I}_t) \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_t). By Proposition 2.5,
  Z_{t+1}^\star = F^\star(Z_t^\star, u_t, w_t) \in F^\star(\mathcal{Z}_t \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, u_t, \mathcal{W}) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.
  By C1, Z_{t+1}^\star \in \widehat{F}(\mathcal{Z}_t, u_t). By (F.4), Z_{t+1}^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_{t+1} \subseteq \mathcal{E}(Y_{t+1}). Hence Z_{t+1}^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_{t+1} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. By C4, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{t+1}) \neq \emptyset.

Thus, on \mathcal{G}, we have Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} for all t \le T. Therefore

\mathcal{G} \subseteq \{Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T\}. \tag{F.5}

Step 4: Probability bound.

Taking complements and using (F.5) and (F.3),

P\bigl(Z_t^\star \notin \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \text{ for some } t \le T \mid A\bigr) \;\le\; P(\mathcal{G}^c \mid A) \;\le\; \Delta.

Hence

P\bigl(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t \le T \mid A\bigr) \;\ge\; 1 - \Delta. \tag{F.6}

Step 5: Unconditional bound.

By the law of total probability,

P(\cdot) = P(\cdot \mid A) P(A) + P(\cdot \mid A^c) P(A^c) \;\ge\; (1-\Delta)(1-\rho) \;\ge\; 1 - \Delta - \rho,

where we use P(A) \ge 1 - \rho. □

F.3 On the Role of C2 and the Estimation Failure \beta_t

Proposition F.1 (Absorption of \beta_t into \alpha_t). If C1 is deterministic and C2 (probabilistic) holds with rate \beta_t, then C5 is satisfied with

\alpha_t^{\mathrm{total}} := \alpha_t^{\mathrm{prop}} + \beta_t,

where \alpha_t^{\mathrm{prop}} accounts for probabilistic failure of \widehat{F} (if any). In particular, if \widehat{F} is deterministic (C1 holds deterministically), one may take \alpha_t^{\mathrm{prop}} = 0.

Proof. The failure event \mathcal{F}_{t+1} = \{Z_{t+1}^\star \notin \mathcal{Z}_{t+1}\} decomposes as

\mathcal{F}_{t+1} = \mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{prop}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{meas}},

where

\mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{prop}} := \{Z_{t+1}^\star \notin \widehat{F}(\mathcal{Z}_t, u_t)\}, \qquad
\mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{meas}} := \{Z_{t+1}^\star \notin \mathcal{E}(Y_{t+1})\}.

Since C1 is deterministic and Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t implies Z_{t+1}^\star \in \widehat{F}(\mathcal{Z}_t, u_t), we have \mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{prop}} \subseteq \mathcal{F}_t, so

P(\mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{prop}} \mid A) \le P(\mathcal{F}_t \mid A) \le \alpha_t^{\mathrm{total}}.

For the measurement term, C2 (probabilistic) gives

P(\mathcal{F}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{meas}} \mid A) \le \beta_{t+1}.

By the union bound,

P(\mathcal{F}_{t+1} \mid A) \le \alpha_t^{\mathrm{total}} + \beta_{t+1}.

Summing over t and absorbing into C6 yields the result. □

Remark F.2 (Minimal budget via C2 alone). If one is willing to dispense with C5 and use C2 (probabilistic) directly with C1 deterministic, the union bound over \{\mathcal{F}_t^{\mathrm{meas}}\}_{t=1}^T gives

P\Bigl(\bigcup_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{F}_t \mid A\Bigr) \le \alpha_0 + \alpha_0' + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \beta_t.

This bypasses the conformal apparatus entirely, at the cost of requiring a per-step estimation-failure bound \beta_t in the probabilistic mode of C2.

Remark F.3 (On conditioning A). The event A = A_{\mathrm{CP}} \cap A_E is a standing assumption: it does not change over time. This is essential for the union bound: if A_{\mathrm{CP}} were to vary per step, one would need a martingale or product-measure argument. The conditioning is preserved across the pathwise induction because A is a global event.

