Appendix G: Full Proofs for Section 9

G.1 Proof of Theorem 9.1 (Action-Set Monotonicity)

Theorem 9.1. For any \mathcal{Z}_1, \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z},

\mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1).

Proof. Assume \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2. Then \mathcal{Z}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Therefore

\bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}_2 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) \;\subseteq\; \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}_1 \cap \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z),

because intersection over a larger index set yields a smaller (or equal) set. By Definition 2.4, this is \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1). □

G.2 Proof of Corollary 9.4 (Monotonicity and Nonnegativity of \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}})

Corollary 9.4. If \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, then

0 \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_2).

Proof. By Theorem 9.1 applied to \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1). By monotonicity of the Borel measure \mu,

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)). \tag{G.1}

By Theorem 9.1 applied to \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1), hence

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1)) \le \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})). \tag{G.2}

Dividing (G.1) and (G.2) by \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) > 0,

\frac{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_2))}{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}))} \;\le\; \frac{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_1))}{\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}))} \;\le\; 1.

Subtracting both sides from 1 and reversing the first inequality,

0 \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_1) \le \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}}(\mathcal{Z}_2). \qquad \square

Remark G.1. The condition \mathcal{Z}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} is essential for nonnegativity. If \mathcal{Z} \not\subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, the ratio \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}))/\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) may exceed 1 and \kappa_{\mathrm{agency}} may be negative. Reference-set membership is a modeling choice, not a mathematical constraint.

G.3 Proof of Proposition 9.5 (Quantitative Monotonicity)

We first restate the two regularity assumptions in a form adequate for the proof.

Assumption 9.A (Lipschitz CBF family). There exists L > 0 and a family \{g_Z\}_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}} of functions g_Z : \mathcal{U} \to \mathbb{R} such that:

(i) \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) = \{u \in \mathcal{U} : g_Z(u) \ge 0\} for every Z \in \mathcal{Z};
(ii) |g_Z(u) - g_{Z'}(u)| \le L \, d_{\mathcal{Z}}(Z, Z') for all Z, Z' \in \mathcal{Z}, all u \in \mathcal{U}.

Assumption 9.B (Level-set regularity). There exists a nondecreasing function \psi : [0,\infty) \to [0,\infty) with \psi(0) = 0 such that for every Z \in \mathcal{Z} and every t \ge 0,

\mu\bigl(\{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) : g_Z(u) < t\}\bigr) \le \psi(t).

Proposition 9.5. Under Assumptions 9.A and 9.B, for any nonempty \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}},

0 \;\le\; \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z})) - \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) \;\le\; \psi\bigl(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr).

Proof. Since \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, Theorem 9.1 gives \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}). Hence

\mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z})) - \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) = \mu\bigl(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr) \ge 0.

This establishes the left inequality.

For the right inequality, fix any Z_0 \in \mathcal{Z} (nonempty by hypothesis). Note that \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, so Z_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and h(Z_0) \ge 0.

We claim

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) \;\subseteq\; \{u \in \mathcal{U} : g_{Z_0}(u) < L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\}. \tag{G.3}

Verification of (G.3). Let u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}).

· Since u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) = \bigcap_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z), in particular u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z_0), so by Assumption 9.A(i), g_{Z_0}(u) \ge 0.
· Since u \notin \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) = \bigcap_{Z' \in \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}} \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z'), there exists Z' \in \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} with u \notin \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z'), i.e., g_{Z'}(u) < 0.
· By Assumption 9.A(ii),
  0 \le g_{Z_0}(u) = g_{Z_0}(u) - g_{Z'}(u) + g_{Z'}(u) < g_{Z_0}(u) - g_{Z'}(u) \le L \, d_{\mathcal{Z}}(Z_0, Z') \le L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}).
  The strict inequality uses g_{Z'}(u) < 0; the final inequality uses Z_0, Z' \in \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}. Hence g_{Z_0}(u) < L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}), establishing (G.3).

Taking measures and applying Assumption 9.B with Z = Z_0 and t = L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}),

\mu\bigl(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) \setminus \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr) \;\le\; \mu\bigl(\{u : g_{Z_0}(u) < L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\}\bigr) \;\le\; \psi\bigl(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr). \qquad \square

Remark G.2 (Non-tightness at \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}). When \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}, the left-hand side is zero but the bound \psi(L \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}})) may be positive. The bound is therefore not tight in this limit. A sharper bound would substitute \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \setminus \mathcal{Z}) or the directed Hausdorff excess

e(\mathcal{Z}, \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) := \sup_{Z' \in \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}} \setminus \mathcal{Z}} \inf_{Z \in \mathcal{Z}} d_{\mathcal{Z}}(Z, Z')

in place of \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}) in (G.3). With this substitution, the bound vanishes as \mathcal{Z} \uparrow \mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{ref}}.

Remark G.3 (Measurability). For \mu(\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z})) to be well-defined, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) must be \Sigma_{\mathcal{U}}-measurable. This is automatic if \mathcal{U} is Polish, g_Z is continuous for each Z, and either \mathcal{Z} is finite or the intersection is countable. For general \mathcal{Z}, measurability of the intersection requires the set-valued map Z \mapsto \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Z) to be measurable in the sense of Definition 2.1. This is assumed.

Remark G.4 (Empty case). If \mathcal{Z} = \emptyset, then \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}) = \mathcal{U} by Convention C.2, and the bound may fail because \mu(\mathcal{U}) is not controlled by \psi. The proposition is therefore stated for nonempty \mathcal{Z}. In applications, \mathcal{Z}_t always contains Z_t^\star by the containment invariant, so nonemptiness is automatic.

