Appendix H: Proof of Proposition 10.4 (Reachability Certificate)

Proposition 10.4. If

\mathfrak{R}^\pi(\mathfrak{I}_0, T) \cap \mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} = \emptyset,

then there exists an admissible safe action at every reachable information state, for all t \le T.

Proof. Let \mathfrak{I} \in \mathfrak{R}^\pi(\mathfrak{I}_0, T). By hypothesis, \mathfrak{I} \notin \mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}}. By Definition 10.3,

\mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} = \{\mathfrak{I}' : \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}'}) = \emptyset\}.

Taking complements,

\mathfrak{I} \notin \mathfrak{I}_{\mathrm{uncertifiable}} \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}}) \neq \emptyset.

Hence there exists u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}}), which is an admissible safe action at \mathfrak{I}. □

Remark H.1. Proposition 10.4 is a recursive availability certificate: it guarantees \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathfrak{I}}) \neq \emptyset at every reachable \mathfrak{I}, but does not by itself guarantee that the resulting trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. The latter requires Proposition 2.5 in conjunction with the containment invariant of Theorem 7.1.

