Appendix I: Full Proof of Proposition 11.1 (Certificate Relations)

Proposition 11.1.

1. D \wedge \{Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t\} \Rightarrow Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}.
2. D \Rightarrow B when \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_t.
3. B \not\Rightarrow P.
4. P \not\Rightarrow B.
5. P \not\Rightarrow D.

Proof.

Claim 1. Assume D : \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} and Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t. Then Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}. □

Claim 2. Assume D and \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_t. Then \operatorname{supp}(b_t) \subseteq \mathcal{Z}_t \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, which is B. □

Claim 3 (B \not\Rightarrow P). Take \mathcal{Z} = \mathbb{R}, \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}} = [0, \infty), Z_t^\star \equiv -1, and b_t = \mathrm{Unif}[0,1]. Then \operatorname{supp}(b_t) = [0,1] \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, so B holds. But P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = P(-1 \in [0,\infty)) = 0. □

Claim 4 (P \not\Rightarrow B). Take Z_t^\star \equiv 1 and b_t = \delta_{-1}. Then P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 1, so P holds. But \operatorname{supp}(b_t) = \{-1\} \not\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, so B fails. □

Claim 5 (P \not\Rightarrow D). Take Z_t^\star \equiv 1 and \mathcal{Z}_t = \{1, -1\}. Then P(Z_t^\star \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}) = 1, so P holds. But -1 \in \mathcal{Z}_t and -1 \notin \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, so \mathcal{Z}_t \not\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}, i.e., D fails. □

Remark I.1 (On the corrected counterexample for B \not\Rightarrow P). An earlier draft proposed b_t = \mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{Z} \setminus \{Z_t^\star\}). This is problematic because if \mathcal{Z} = \mathbb{R} and Z_t^\star \in \mathrm{cl}(\mathcal{Z} \setminus \{Z_t^\star\}), the closure of the support includes Z_t^\star, making the support-containment statement ambiguous. The corrected counterexample uses a compactly supported belief on [0,1] with the true state at -1, which separates support and truth topologically.

Remark I.2 (Independent layers). Claims 3–5 show that D, P, B are logically independent in the sense that no two of them are equivalent in general. The only implication that holds universally is D \Rightarrow P (with probability at least the containment probability) and D \Rightarrow B (under support-containment of \mathcal{Z}_t).

